1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,943
This is Capybara Onsen.

2
00:00:01,943 --> 00:00:04,920
More and more of its guests are not people.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:09,075
They are AI agents, booking a soak on someone else's behalf.

4
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:14,621
The onsen is about to ship an accessibility upgrade to its booking form.

5
00:00:14,621 --> 00:00:17,976
To a person, the new version looks exactly the same.

6
00:00:17,976 --> 00:00:19,719
Not one pixel is different.

7
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:23,369
But an AI agent does not look at pixels.

8
00:00:23,369 --> 00:00:25,570
It reads the accessibility tree.

9
00:00:25,570 --> 00:00:29,627
And there, the two versions could hardly be more different:

10
00:00:29,627 --> 00:00:34,646
unnamed controls on one side, buttons with names and states on the other.

11
00:00:35,546 --> 00:00:38,631
So what does the upgrade do to the AI guests?

12
00:00:38,631 --> 00:00:41,099
We started out expecting it to help.

13
00:00:41,099 --> 00:00:46,171
Two small pilots disagreed: every guest booked correctly on both versions.

14
00:00:46,171 --> 00:00:50,352
So before the real run, we registered a different prediction:

15
00:00:50,352 --> 00:00:53,231
bookings will hold, and tokens will go up.

16
00:00:54,131 --> 00:00:57,790
Both versions sit behind one Flagsmith feature flag.

17
00:00:57,790 --> 00:01:03,278
A Flagsmith experiment splits the guests fifty-fifty and watches four metrics:

18
00:01:03,278 --> 00:01:07,219
correct bookings, completed bookings, steps, and tokens.

19
00:01:08,119 --> 00:01:11,509
The guests are language models driving real browsers,

20
00:01:11,509 --> 00:01:14,707
with no hint about which version they were served.

21
00:01:14,707 --> 00:01:16,945
Watch this one on the classic form.

22
00:01:16,945 --> 00:01:18,928
Its preferred time is sold out,

23
00:01:18,928 --> 00:01:22,766
and the only clue in the markup is a missing pointer cursor.

24
00:01:22,766 --> 00:01:25,836
It clicks, nothing happens, and it clicks again.

25
00:01:25,836 --> 00:01:27,755
This is the worst case we saw.

26
00:01:27,755 --> 00:01:30,506
Most detours were one or two wasted clicks.

27
00:01:31,406 --> 00:01:35,555
On the accessible form, the same time is marked as disabled.

28
00:01:35,555 --> 00:01:39,843
The guest reads that, and goes straight to the next free slot.

29
00:01:40,743 --> 00:01:43,552
The experiment itself is run by a coding agent,

30
00:01:43,552 --> 00:01:46,719
using a skill we built on the Flagsmith command line.

31
00:01:46,719 --> 00:01:50,843
Before anything goes live, it checks that every event really arrives,

32
00:01:50,843 --> 00:01:53,413
and that every user still gets the control.

33
00:01:53,413 --> 00:01:56,939
Then it stops, and waits for a person to approve the start.

34
00:01:57,839 --> 00:02:01,183
Three hundred guests later, this is Flagsmith's analysis.

35
00:02:02,083 --> 00:02:06,279
Correct bookings: 156 out of 156 on the classic form,

36
00:02:06,279 --> 00:02:09,525
and 144 out of 144 on the accessible one.

37
00:02:09,525 --> 00:02:11,188
No difference at all.

38
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:13,167
Steps went down a little.

39
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:17,601
But tokens per guest went up by almost fourteen percent,

40
00:02:17,601 --> 00:02:20,055
and that guardrail is breached.

41
00:02:20,955 --> 00:02:21,273
Why?

42
00:02:21,273 --> 00:02:24,688
A capable agent recovers from its mistakes,

43
00:02:24,688 --> 00:02:28,024
so the bookings come out right either way.

44
00:02:28,024 --> 00:02:32,393
But on the classic form, 24 guests took a detour first.

45
00:02:32,393 --> 00:02:35,015
On the accessible form, none did.

46
00:02:35,015 --> 00:02:40,654
And yet the accessible page takes longer to read, on every single step.

47
00:02:40,654 --> 00:02:42,958
Fewer mistakes, more reading.

48
00:02:42,958 --> 00:02:44,308
The reading wins.

49
00:02:45,208 --> 00:02:48,640
So when the agent tries to ship, the skill refuses.

50
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:51,062
A breached guardrail needs a person.

51
00:02:51,062 --> 00:02:54,561
A person was shown the result and accepted the cost:

52
00:02:54,561 --> 00:02:58,329
the upgrade is for people, and agents book just as well.

53
00:02:58,329 --> 00:03:00,751
Flagsmith rolled it out to everyone.

54
00:03:01,651 --> 00:03:03,438
The skill is small on purpose.

55
00:03:03,438 --> 00:03:06,416
Its instructions are about sixteen hundred tokens,

56
00:03:06,416 --> 00:03:08,857
and its commands print one fact per line.

57
00:03:08,857 --> 00:03:12,252
A fresh agent, working from the skill and a short prompt,

58
00:03:12,252 --> 00:03:14,634
ran the whole job in fifteen tool calls.

59
00:03:15,534 --> 00:03:17,515
And you can check it yourself.

60
00:03:17,515 --> 00:03:21,412
On a fresh clone, one command starts open-source Flagsmith,

61
00:03:21,412 --> 00:03:24,978
the booking site, and a hundred and twenty new guests.

62
00:03:24,978 --> 00:03:26,035
We ran it twice.

63
00:03:26,035 --> 00:03:29,932
Both times, no guest on the accessible form wasted a click,

64
00:03:29,932 --> 00:03:31,913
and the token bill was higher.

65
00:03:32,813 --> 00:03:38,063
One experiment, a prediction registered in advance, and a result nobody scripted.
